  "notes": "The lane's other defining bassist. `confidence` is **low** — the band role and the instruments are sourced, the parametric detail is inference. Two claims drive the whole model. The first is harmonic: \"he moves *with* the reharmonisation rather than under it — when the pianist re-voices a repeated melody, the bass changes root, so the same 4-bar melody sits over three different bass paths. Chord-tone-driven, rarely root-only\", which is a `followRootsProb` of 0.2 and a chord voicing pushed up to 54 to clear a bass that reaches 52 — \"played electric bass (5- and 6-string), sometimes distorted or octave-doubled, with a wide register and a soloistic role; also upright on the acoustic material. Long sustained notes alternate with dense 16th passages; no 808.\" The second is the interlock: \"he pairs with Chris Dave; the bass fills the gaps the displaced snare leaves, so the bass and drums together imply the backbeat that neither states.\" So the drums carry a 13–27 ms displacement and a 0.64 ghost probability with the hat mostly withheld, and the bass carries the highest anticipation in this batch — it is answering the snare rather than following the kick. \"As a composer he writes for strings and gospel choir alongside the band, so model a bass-plus-orchestra variant with no drums at all\", which is why the countermelody is a sustain pad and the arrangement runs long open sections. ⚠ Every drum number and every melody figure here is INFERRED. ⚠ His drum grammar is Chris Dave's by the research's own statement; this model states it at its own numbers rather than copying that one, and the difference between them is authored in the bass, not invented in the kit.",
